/**
 * A string of Unicode text. The stored representation is platform-dependent.
 *
 * A string is a sequence of **code points**, and that is the unit everything counting or indexing into it
 * uses: `length` counts code points, and `substring`, `take`, `drop` and `indexOf` all index in them. The
 * unit is a property of the text itself rather than of a platform's storage, so a length or an index means
 * the same number on every target — a string held as UTF-16 and the same string held as UTF-8 have the same
 * length, and no slice can ever cut a character in half.
 *
 * What a code point is *not* is a user-perceived character: a combining sequence (`e` followed by an acute
 * accent) is more than one, and so is an emoji built from several. Use `length` as a measure of content,
 * not of the width something occupies on a display.
 *
 * A string also carries a compile-time **size**: the range of lengths the compiler knows it may have,
 * counted in exactly the unit `length` returns. Because that unit is the code point, a size means the same
 * thing on every target, so a `where` precondition written over it holds wherever the program is compiled.
 */
type String
